Activity 8 — What we'll build
Azure Key Vault
      ↓
Certificate
      ↓
Automation Runbook
      ↓
Check expiry date
      ↓
30-day threshold
      ↓
Slack alert if expiring


Step 1 — Create the Key Vault

In Azure Portal:

Create a resource → Key Vault

Use:

Resource group: devvrat-test
Key vault name: something globally unique, e.g. kv-devvrat-cert
Region: East US
Pricing tier: Standard
Keep the default security settings unless Azure requires otherwise.

Step 2 — Create a test certificate

Open your Key Vault kv-devvrat-cert.

Go to:

Objects → Certificates → Generate/Import → Generate

Set:

Method of Certificate Creation: Generate
Certificate Name: TestCertificate
Type of Certificate Authority (CA): Self-signed certificate
Subject: CN=test.devvrat.local
Validity Period: 90 days
Content Type: PKCS #12
Leave the other options at their defaults.

Why 90 days?

We need a real certificate with an expiry date so our Runbook can calculate:

Certificate expiry

Step 3 — Give costopt permission to read the certificate

We want the system-assigned managed identity of Automation Account costopt to read certificates from Key Vault kv-devvrat-cert.

In the Key Vault:

kv-devvrat-cert → Access control (IAM) → Add → Add role assignment

Choose:

Role: Key Vault Certificates Officer is not what we need.
Use Key Vault Reader if available for your setup.

Then:

Next → Assign access to: Managed identity → Select members

We only want the Automation Account to read certificate metadata, particularly:

Certificate name
Expiration date

It doesn't need permission to modify or delete the certificate.   


Step 4 — Create the certificate-check Runbook

Go to:

Automation Account costopt → Runbooks → Create a runbook

Set:

Name: CertificateExpiryCheck
Runbook type: PowerShell
Runtime: 7.2
Description: Checks Key Vault certificate expiry and reports certificates nearing expiration\

It simply:

Connect to Azure
      ↓
Read certificates from kv-devvrat-cert
      ↓
Get expiration date
      ↓
Calculate days remaining
      ↓
≤ 30 days → WARNING
> 30 days → OK

Don't publish yet. Save it and run it in the Test pane first.

Since your certificate currently has plenty of validity remaining, we should expect something like:

Certificate: TestCertificate
Expires: ...
Days remaining: ...
STATUS: OK - Certificate has more than 30 days remaining.

Certificate expiry check completed.

Step 5 — Add Slack alerting

We'll modify the Runbook so:

Certificate >30 days → Slack says OK
Certificate ≤30 days → Slack says WARNING
Certificate ≤7 days → Slack says CRITICAL

We can reuse your existing encrypted Automation variable:

SlackWebhookUrl